WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:05.800
All the way back in 2016, we learned that
a new Armored Core game was in the works.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:12.660
So back then I thought.."man..I really should
go and play all the old Armored Core games.

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:18.380
I need to understand why people love this
series. How many games could there even be?

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:24.420
5? 6?"
But then I realised the truth. There are 15

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:29.850
unique Armored Core games. And that's not
even counting the 3 PSP remakes, or the multiple

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:36.230
discs some of these games have. Not to mention
the arena modes. The branching mission paths.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:41.860
Hard mode, NG+, optional objectives, the lore
that makes even my head spin -

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:48.070
These are complex, challenging, and extremely
intricate games. And while they never really

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:53.210
hit mainstream appeal, it was Armored Core
and its dedicated fans that kept From Software

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:59.789
afloat for many, many years. And then, sadly,
it was those same fans who had to watch their

00:00:59.789 --> 00:01:06.101
favourite franchise take a back seat when Souls became From Software's breadwinner instead.

00:01:06.101 --> 00:01:08.794
So I wanted to experience these games the

00:01:08.794 --> 00:01:16.409
same way the old fans did. Not with emulators,
but with old hardware, and all of the authenticity

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:25.290
and all of the frustration that comes with that. So many
Ebay purchases and over $2000 US dollars later,

00:01:25.290 --> 00:01:33.549
I had them. Three used consoles, two retro
capture devices, and 18 absurdly expensive

00:01:33.549 --> 00:01:39.180
old video games. I was ready.
So I went back. To the first Armored Core,

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:44.990
released all the way back in 1997 ...
... And I was thrown straight into the deep end.

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:48.390
"You must battle against the opponent AC and survive.

00:01:48.390 --> 00:01:54.710
If you survive, you will be considered
a Raven. That's all. Good luck".

00:01:54.710 --> 00:02:00.149
This is how Armored Core begins. With you
in a fight for your life, taking fire

00:02:00.149 --> 00:02:05.789
as you frantically run around the arena and
attempt to puzzle out the controls. There's

00:02:05.789 --> 00:02:11.760
literally no guide or tips - nothing..just
From Software saying "ehh, they'll figure

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:16.010
it out".
One by one, I stumble through the controls and try to commit them to memory.

00:02:16.010 --> 00:02:20.780
X is boost, Square to shoot, Triangle to change weapon..

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:27.040
but when it comes to aiming: something feels wrong.
Because these enemies can fly, right? which means

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:32.530
I need a way to aim up or down at them. And
since moving forwards and backwards are already

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:41.099
bound here..that means..yeah. So looking 
UP and DOWN is bound to the LEFT and RIGHT

00:02:41.099 --> 00:02:47.970
back triggers. And that sucks.
Ima be honest - even after beating all of

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:52.800
these Armored Core games, I'm sitting here
now, writing this script, and I honestly still

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:58.010
could not tell you from memory which back
trigger moves the camera up or down. If there

00:02:58.010 --> 00:03:03.310
was ever a time I needed to look up at something,
there was a 50% chance I would look down at

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:04.519
the floor instead.

00:03:04.519 --> 00:03:11.090
But somehow, I push through, and I do eventually
learn how to get good. These games will simply

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:17.870
not allow you to progress if you don't. It's
classic From Software: even all those years ago. They throw you in the

00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:29.800
deep end, and expect the best from you. Will
you sink? Or swim?

00:03:29.800 --> 00:03:35.909
This is the Raven's Nest. Here, you can assemble
your mech - but you have no new parts. So

00:03:35.909 --> 00:03:40.280
you go to the shop, where you have no money.
So you end up at the mission board, where

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:47.540
you become a wage slave to the two biggest
corporations - "Chrome" and "Murakumo", who

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:51.720
are in a desperate battle with eachother for
supremacy.

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:56.920
But your first mission is from the city guard,
who are very angry..because some workers are

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:02.290
protesting in a nearby factory. And since
it's not a good look for the city guard to

00:04:02.290 --> 00:04:13.220
use lethal force on..you know: innocent civilians:
they give you 14,000 credits to do it instead.

00:04:13.220 --> 00:04:20.120
The workers' crude cranes and worker MTs are
no match for your armored war machine, and

00:04:20.120 --> 00:04:26.840
you walk away with 14,000 credits - Except..wait,
you have to pay for the bullets you used on

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:31.910
those evil protesters, and you'll also need
to repair the damage you took during the mission

00:04:31.910 --> 00:04:34.820
as well.
But don't worry! There are always more striking

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:39.250
workers who need to be taught a lesson, and
there's always someone else's dirty work to

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:44.789
profit from. I was making pretty good money
- that is, until the third mission, where

00:04:44.789 --> 00:04:50.440
I was tasked with rescuing a truck filled
with chemical weaponry. I proceeded to promptly

00:04:50.440 --> 00:04:56.900
blow up the truck - and I assumed I would
get to just try again..but no: instead, the

00:04:56.900 --> 00:05:03.870
mission completed, and gave me my reward:
Minus 1.4k for ammunition, minus 4.4k for

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:11.090
repairs and minus 200 credits in compensation
for that poor civilian's car I crushed in

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:16.780
the heat of battle. So...I hadn't just failed
the mission: I had actually lost money attempting

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:22.449
it. I knew I was going to be morally bankrupt
going into this game, but turns out you can

00:05:22.449 --> 00:05:26.576
be financially bankrupt as well, if you play
poorly enough.

00:05:26.576 --> 00:05:32.949
Looking back at my footage now, I realise
I was scared to spend credits, probably because

00:05:32.949 --> 00:05:39.000
new parts and their stats were so overwhelming.
Eventually though, I would realise that..you

00:05:39.000 --> 00:05:44.960
can't go too wrong spending credits in these
games. If it's expensive, chances are it's

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:49.430
an upgrade: and even if you regret buying
it..well you can always sell those parts back

00:05:49.430 --> 00:05:54.950
to the shop for the same price you bought
them at, and just buy something else instead.

00:05:54.950 --> 00:06:00.440
This is a game that won't stop you making
mistakes. BUT it won't always punish you for

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:04.330
those mistakes either.
So I had no idea what I wanted to buy from

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:09.970
the shop, but I had heard whispers about two
legendary weapons that existed in this game,

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:15.800
and I knew I NEEDED to try them. I needed
to figure out why they were so popular.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:21.479
It's called the "Karasawa", an energy rifle that you actually can't get in the shop. 

00:06:21.479 --> 00:06:25.480
Instead, it was one of the many hidden parts in this game. These were

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:29.039
parts that you actually had to hunt down,
and pick up during missions.

00:06:29.039 --> 00:06:31.569
I had read somewhere that the Karasawa was

00:06:31.569 --> 00:06:36.270
hidden behind some explosive barrels in an
early mission - and it was easy enough to

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:42.509
find.
This thing was insane. It DRAMATICALLY increased

00:06:42.509 --> 00:06:47.340
my firepower, and most enemies would die in
just a few shots! But it wasn't without its

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:53.130
downsides: for one, the Karasawa was extremely
heavy - so I realised I needed to buy heavy

00:06:53.130 --> 00:07:00.080
legs in order to accommodate it. The Karasawa
also has limited ammo, so I equipped some

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:06.770
light missiles as backup. It's really interesting
how just changing one part can fundamentally

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:12.650
change your build: new parts will introduce
new problems that need to be solved, but by solving

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:18.330
these your knowledge about parts
and stats will just naturally increase. And I think

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:22.690
that's really cool.
I also realised that the Karasawa was an energy

00:07:22.690 --> 00:07:28.129
weapon, so it costs energy to fire. That meant
I needed to take a closer look at my generator,

00:07:28.129 --> 00:07:33.530
and I needed to decide whether I needed a
generator with high energy regen, or a generator

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:39.940
with high capacity. High regen would make
me better in prolonged fights, but high capacity

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:49.060
would allow me to fire a ton more shots and
overwhelm enemies in the short term. Again,

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:54.800
it's really amazing that Armored Core allows
you to change something as fundamental as

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:58.580
energy regeneration.
The final thing I learned from the Karasawa,

00:07:58.580 --> 00:08:05.440
was that its energy ammo was actually free. And in
the early game when you're strapped for cash:

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:10.430
that's an amazing thing. And so I was learning
so many lessons that would serve me well in

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:12.902
all of the Armored Core games to follow.

00:08:12.902 --> 00:08:19.305
Now, these parts were powerful - but it's
not like they made the game easy.

00:08:19.305 --> 00:08:26.430
Looking up and down was still a pain, some
missions had VERY unclear objectives, and there

00:08:26.430 --> 00:08:32.200
were so many missions where I'd spend ages
navigating mazes of corridors with agonisingly slow turn

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:36.640
speeds..only to die, or run out of ammo, right
at the end.

00:08:36.640 --> 00:08:43.320
So most of the time, I was just craving single
combat against enemy ACs. Those were my favourite fights.

00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:50.010
And if the fight had some questionable voice
acting to laugh at, well: even better. "you..you're

00:08:50.010 --> 00:09:00.310
a raven? I don't know anything about those
things here. *explodes*"

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:07.370
There was also a frustrating moment at the end where I
died to the final boss, and then I got a

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:14.180
unique cutscene. But this wasn't just a unique
cutscene..it was a..game over screen. So for

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:21.079
the first time in HOURS of play, I discovered
this, and it booted me back to my last save,

00:09:21.079 --> 00:09:26.820
which 
I had made SO LONG ago. So it was just so much fun having to redo all these missions again 

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:31.924
and try to remember what I had changed in my build..that was..great. /s

00:09:31.924 --> 00:09:37.990
And you might say say "well you should have just saved!" but this was PS1 era, you know? Saving took time.

00:09:37.990 --> 00:09:45.500
Menus were slow. So I didn't bother to save until I had this experience.
But I pushed through. This last level I was

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:51.240
stuck on was tricky, because it required me
to clear rooms and rooms of enemies really efficiently,

00:09:51.240 --> 00:09:56.510
so I had to make sure to manage my health and ammo so
that I had the resources required for the

00:09:56.510 --> 00:10:04.240
final boss. It was fun overcoming it.
Except this was NOT the final boss. And I

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:08.890
wasn't done. Not by a long shot.
The true final mission takes place at the

00:10:08.890 --> 00:10:13.899
Raven's Nest headquarters, where a mysterious
band has broken in, and left behind a set

00:10:13.899 --> 00:10:20.130
of floating mines. Now, the Raven's Nest itself
wants you to come in and dismantle them.

00:10:20.130 --> 00:10:25.010
The first part of the mission is simple enough.
I had to take out one small room of five easy

00:10:25.010 --> 00:10:30.649
enemies, making sure I used my blade to conserve
ammo. Because the next room was considerably

00:10:30.649 --> 00:10:37.740
more difficult, as it was so packed full of enemies
that my playstation lagged uncontrollably.

00:10:37.740 --> 00:10:45.270
This room was tricky, but easier with some
practice. And..I had plenty of opportunities to practice.

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:51.420
Because while your ammunition and health is
restored after completing this second room, if you fail after

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:56.430
this point, you'll get game over and have
to go back all the way back to your last save.

00:10:56.430 --> 00:11:02.270
So for part 2 of the mission, you find yourself
in this vast, square hallway.

00:11:02.270 --> 00:11:05.860
Lucky for you, there's
a way up. Unlucky for you, the devs forgot

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:11.820
what game they were making halfway through
and decided to start making a platformer instead.

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:16.760
But this isn't doodle jump, this is
armored core. Remember: looking up and down

00:11:16.760 --> 00:11:22.550
in this game is excruciating..and this hallway
is vertical! So you have to look up to where

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:27.670
you're going, and then you have to look DOWN
to make sure you have your landing secured. Every time.

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:33.889
Because all of these platforms are moving.
They're moving in different directions as well. And all at different

00:11:33.889 --> 00:11:39.680
speeds! Some will even run into eachother
and slide you off, which is great! And then there's the turrets

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:45.410
attached to the top and bottom of many platforms,
which will give you knockback and stagger

00:11:45.410 --> 00:11:52.130
and damage while you attempt your ascent. They don't do
much damage, and they don't take many hits

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:57.570
to defeat..but oh man - you're going to really miss
that HP and ammo when you finally get to the

00:11:57.570 --> 00:12:04.649
top. Because the worst is yet to come. This
is just a frustrating, time-consuming interlude

00:12:04.649 --> 00:12:10.920
for you to enjoy every - single - time you
have to re-do this mission. And unless you're

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:16.991
god or using save states on an emulator, you
WILL have to re-do this mission...and that's

00:12:16.991 --> 00:12:21.190
because of what..or who comes next.

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:23.190
Name of AC: Nine-ball

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:27.190
If you had ever looked at the "ranking" menu in the raven's nest,

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:31.930
you would have seen a list of all the enemy
ACs in the game, and Nineball is the top ranked

00:12:31.930 --> 00:12:38.140
AC, piloted by someone named "Hustler one".
And he's top rank for a reason. Just watch

00:12:38.140 --> 00:12:52.470
this attempt of me fighting him.
But not to worry - after Getting Over It 20 minutes later, I was

00:12:52.470 --> 00:13:09.260
back for round two. Which went like this:
and on, and on, and on it went, until I simply

00:13:09.260 --> 00:13:14.639
gave up. Nineball is overpowered. Literally
- he actually has something unlocked called

00:13:14.639 --> 00:13:20.399
"Human Plus", which is an overpowered state
you can unlock if you go 50,000 credits in

00:13:20.399 --> 00:13:27.640
debt. Which is cool, but I didn't do that! So Nineball can fly
better than me, he's faster than me, he

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:32.399
has better weaponry than me, he approaches
from above with the high ground, and he forces

00:13:32.399 --> 00:13:38.469
you to fight by aiming up and down because of the nature of the vertical arena that you're in.

00:13:38.469 --> 00:13:45.830
Plus, you've
already been weakened mentally and physically by playing Jump King for the last 10 minutes every time you attempt this.

00:13:45.830 --> 00:13:53.709
So.. I decided not to fight at all. I decided
to run for my fucking life, past this absolute

00:13:53.709 --> 00:13:58.899
terror, to see if I could just get past him
and complete the mission that way.

00:13:58.899 --> 00:14:04.459
On my first escape run, I choked, and I missed
the tiny hole you're supposed to fly into.

00:14:04.459 --> 00:14:10.430
And then on my second try......I made it.
But somehow, Nineball managed to get in after me.

00:14:14.713 --> 00:14:18.666
Back then I guess I had
one thought in my mind here - and that was

00:14:18.666 --> 00:14:26.870
to flee. Down the corridor in terror, away
from this black and red AC that

00:14:26.949 --> 00:14:31.940
had been killing me all day.
The AI core that you destroy at the end is

00:14:31.940 --> 00:14:37.160
somehow a part of Nine-ball's consciousness, and you
destroy it. Mission complete.

00:14:43.140 --> 00:14:46.170
Thank god I don't have to fight that guy again.

00:14:46.170 --> 00:14:51.360
Next up was Armored Core: Project Phantasma
- which was released only a few months after

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:56.060
the first Armored Core! They were able to
do this because..while Project Phantasma can

00:14:56.060 --> 00:15:01.490
still be played as its own standalone game,
it's perhaps more accurate to call this an

00:15:01.490 --> 00:15:06.670
"expansion". The game essentially plays the
same as the first one, except with new missions,

00:15:06.670 --> 00:15:12.490
new parts, and some rebalancing to old parts.
You can even transfer your save from the first

00:15:12.490 --> 00:15:18.740
game OVER to this one if you want to continue
with your previous AC, and all its parts,

00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:22.380
which is really cool.
And this is basically how all the Armored

00:15:22.380 --> 00:15:28.490
Core games work. The easiest way to understand how they're all connected is when they're arranged like

00:15:28.490 --> 00:15:34.910
this. Each row represents a "generation" of
Armored Core games, where your save can continue

00:15:34.910 --> 00:15:44.000
within the generation. There's gen 1 for the PS1,
gen 2, 3, and 3.5 for the PS2, and then gen

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:53.610
4 and 5 for the PS3 and Xbox 360. Gen 1-3.5 play quite
similarly, but the series would eventually

00:15:53.610 --> 00:16:01.108
change radically in generation 4, and then
again in generation 5, as you'll see later on.

00:16:01.108 --> 00:16:04.860
Anyway: I wanted to try a new build in Project Phantasma.

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:11.779
And I'd since learned that the most defining
part of your build, are your legs. It's kind of like the

00:16:11.779 --> 00:16:18.520
RPG equivalent of choosing a class. They determine
how fast you move, how fast you turn, the

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:24.700
nature of your movement, and perhaps most
importantly: how much weight you can carry.

00:16:24.700 --> 00:16:30.649
First I tried some tread legs. Tread legs
allow you to carry extremely heavy loads,

00:16:30.649 --> 00:16:36.889
and even allow you to fire heavy back weapons
WHILE moving or flying, which no other leg

00:16:36.889 --> 00:16:44.699
part can do. However, this comes at the cost
of your movement and flying ability aaand..one

00:16:44.699 --> 00:16:52.770
mission was enough to decide that this was not
for me. Like..imagine completing every mission

00:16:52.770 --> 00:16:58.250
in the game going this slowly.
What I ended up settling on, were quad legs.

00:16:58.250 --> 00:17:04.429
Quad legs are like tread legs in the sense
that they allow you to fire heavy back weapons

00:17:04.429 --> 00:17:10.610
while moving - though you can't fly while
you do so. But unlike tread legs, quad legs

00:17:10.610 --> 00:17:17.929
exist on a bit of a spectrum, where you can
equip heavy load bearing quads, but also fast,

00:17:17.929 --> 00:17:21.410
lightweight quads as well. I opted for the latter.

00:17:21.410 --> 00:17:26.669
Now lightweight ACs are kinda hard to put
together, because there's a lower weight limit,

00:17:26.669 --> 00:17:32.049
which prevents you from choosing stronger,
weightier parts. So putting this build together

00:17:32.049 --> 00:17:37.950
would require a lot of trial and error..and
I realised that there's no better place for

00:17:37.950 --> 00:17:43.419
this build process, than in a place called the arena.
In the arena 

00:17:43.419 --> 00:17:48.799
you fight exclusively against enemy ACs in
one-on-one combat, ranking up and earning

00:17:48.799 --> 00:17:55.830
credits as you go. And while enemy ACs are
some of the toughest enemies you can face..there

00:17:55.830 --> 00:18:01.530
are no ammo costs here, so you can only really
benefit from these fights. But best of all

00:18:01.530 --> 00:18:10.231
it teaches you about different enemy builds, and it shows you what works and what doesn't work and it shows you the limit of your own build.

00:18:10.231 --> 00:18:16.190
It's the perfect space to refine and adjust your
AC into a machine capable of taking on some

00:18:16.190 --> 00:18:21.770
of the hardest fights in the game, and that's
exactly what my build quickly became: A fast,

00:18:21.770 --> 00:18:31.090
lightweight quad with machine gun arms, and
ammo canisters on its back. A build designed to do one thing really well, and that was 

00:18:31.090 --> 00:18:37.580
to run circles around opponents, staying out
of their FCS lock box, and keeping them in

00:18:37.580 --> 00:18:40.500
my own.
It's hard to explain just how satisfying it

00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:46.010
is to refine your build in these games, and
to see it all come together perfectly at the

00:18:46.010 --> 00:18:54.390
end. You solve weight problems, energy problems,
ammo problems..you compromise on some parts,

00:18:54.390 --> 00:18:58.850
stand your ground on others..and if you're
lucky, you'll end up with a build that 

00:18:58.850 --> 00:19:04.090
is entirely your own. You know
how to play it. You know what it's weaknesses

00:19:04.090 --> 00:19:09.970
are. You know its strengths. You know exactly
what challenges went into creating it, and

00:19:09.970 --> 00:19:14.370
it's yours.
So when I went back into missions and I realised

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:19.809
machine gun ammo costs were going to put me
DEEP into debt, it was easy for me to tweak

00:19:19.809 --> 00:19:25.250
my build into a more cost efficient one for
most missions, because I understood how to undo certain parts of my build.

00:19:25.250 --> 00:19:36.120
But for the final mission, I wheeled out my lightweight, 
expensive AC killer, and went to town. The

00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:41.630
final fight was against Stinger, who's..a
bit of a joke, honestly - at least compared

00:19:41.630 --> 00:19:46.559
to Nineball in the previous
game. His strategy was to shoot out so many

00:19:46.559 --> 00:19:52.510
rainbow bullets that my FPS would drop to
unplayable levels..and it almost worked - because

00:19:52.510 --> 00:19:57.940
I actually ran out of ammo in this fight,
and assumed that I'd lost. So at this point

00:19:57.940 --> 00:20:03.070
I actually gave up and went close to him so
that he could kill me faster and get it over

00:20:03.070 --> 00:20:11.650
with, but then this happened: His own missiles
dealt the final piece of damage to himself!

00:20:11.650 --> 00:20:17.870
Pretty fitting way for this guy to go out.
My AC gloriously flies into the roof, and

00:20:17.870 --> 00:20:23.000
that's the end of Project Phantasma.
I felt like I was getting pretty good at the

00:20:23.000 --> 00:20:28.410
series..but From Software were watching - just
waiting to tear me back down again. Because

00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:35.080
the next game was "Armored Core: Master of
Arena", and I legitimately felt dread when

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:40.960
I put in the disk, loaded up the first mission..and
watched this cutscene.

00:21:07.169 --> 00:21:13.500
Nineball was back. And this time, it was personal..on
a few levels! Because..I wanted revenge on

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:20.679
Nineball for him shaming me in the first game
in front of all my YouTube viewers, and apparently

00:21:20.679 --> 00:21:24.084
my character wanted revenge as well.

00:21:27.029 --> 00:21:32.159
Lana is your contact at the Raven's Nest,
and mail from her reads "When we first met

00:21:32.159 --> 00:21:36.480
on the network, you mentioned becoming a Raven
because there was someone you wanted to kill.

00:21:36.480 --> 00:21:43.120
AC Name: Nineball. Pilot: Hustler One. Top
ranked AC in the Arena. That is all we know."

00:21:43.120 --> 00:21:48.110
She comments that anyone who attempts to face
Nine-Ball in battle will surely be destroyed,

00:21:48.110 --> 00:21:53.150
but that if we're really serious about this,
then she can arrange it so that we will

00:21:53.150 --> 00:21:56.760
one day fight him.
So Lana immediately passes us a mission from

00:21:56.760 --> 00:22:02.850
PROGTECH, who manufacture AC parts. They want
us to put down some rampaging robots in their

00:22:02.850 --> 00:22:09.039
factory, and in return they'll give us some
free parts, and sponsor us in the Arena.

00:22:09.039 --> 00:22:14.530
What follows is this glorious mix of mission
and arena gameplay. Sometimes your mission

00:22:14.530 --> 00:22:19.140
board will be completely empty, and all you
can access is the arena, which you'll need

00:22:19.140 --> 00:22:23.720
to progress in in order to unlock missions
again. And then sometimes it's the reverse,

00:22:23.720 --> 00:22:28.100
where the arena is locked off because there's
an urgent mission you need to complete for

00:22:28.100 --> 00:22:34.950
your sponsor. And it's this balance...the
way that arena and mission gameplay is interwoven,

00:22:34.950 --> 00:22:41.410
that makes Master of Arena easily rank in
my top 3 Armored Core games. Because..the

00:22:41.410 --> 00:22:45.750
pacing here is just phenomenal. In the other
games, missions would sometimes feel like

00:22:45.750 --> 00:22:52.530
a grind; and doing the arena often felt like
an optional side-quest. But in this game

00:22:52.530 --> 00:22:57.350
missions had me feeling like I was strengthening
my relationship with my sponsor - and when

00:22:57.350 --> 00:23:03.450
I progressed in the Arena, I knew it was in
pursuit of a goal: to rise through the ranks,

00:23:03.450 --> 00:23:09.010
so that I could one day have the right to
challenge Nine-Ball, who is formidable in

00:23:09.010 --> 00:23:14.450
the story of this game, just as he's formidable
in a gameplay sense as well.

00:23:14.450 --> 00:23:20.799
So I continued to chip away at the missions,
and in the arena, I was a demon - I was this red AC

00:23:20.799 --> 00:23:28.740
wielding the Karasawa and the moonlight sword and some really fast legs. These legs were so fast that I didn't

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:35.420
really need my boosters, which meant that I had more
energy for shooting my Karasawa while grounded.

00:23:35.420 --> 00:23:39.600
With this build, I flew through the arena
- and if I was ever having trouble with an

00:23:39.600 --> 00:23:49.870
opponent, it would be because they were flying. But if they were flying, I'd take them to the garage - an
arena level which limited their flight capabilities,

00:23:49.870 --> 00:24:00.270
and kept them on the ground with me so that I didn't have to press the accursed R2 and L2 triggers.
I was feeling pretty confident..and then the final

00:24:00.270 --> 00:24:03.810
fight against nineball arrived.

00:24:18.415 --> 00:24:22.671
I thought this fight went pretty well! Got him on the first try...

00:24:22.671 --> 00:24:25.242
Except that wasn't the end.

00:25:08.080 --> 00:25:19.460
Phase two has you fighting..TWO nineballs.
And that's exactly as difficult as it sounds.

00:25:19.460 --> 00:25:25.159
The reason there's two, is because it's been
revealed that the Nineballs are able to be

00:25:25.159 --> 00:25:31.630
mass produced, and they actually aren't piloted by
a human - instead, they're controlled by a

00:25:31.630 --> 00:25:38.820
highly advanced combat AI that thinks it can
rebuild the world by manufacturing conflict,

00:25:38.820 --> 00:25:46.640
as long as they're the strongest Raven. Which
is why it has to take you out. Which it does. Repeatedly.

00:25:49.349 --> 00:25:52.260
Quickly I realised that it's extremely important

00:25:52.260 --> 00:25:56.450
that you do well in the first fight against
the single Nineball, because your health and

00:25:56.450 --> 00:26:01.710
ammo that remains is what you'll need to take
on the two at once.

00:26:01.710 --> 00:26:06.530
And after a ton of time consuming resets,
I realised there was an upper level to the

00:26:06.530 --> 00:26:13.594
dual nineball fight, which could be used to
potentially separate the two and help you

00:26:13.594 --> 00:26:24.096
fight one at a time.
And finally..I was victorious.

00:26:31.982 --> 00:26:35.919
There's a certain kind of absurd, giddy dread

00:26:35.919 --> 00:26:41.679
that I've only ever experienced playing From
Soft games. That feeling that even though

00:26:41.679 --> 00:26:45.984
you've pushed yourself to the limit, the challenge
actually isn't over yet.

00:27:31.970 --> 00:27:38.690
This third phase is extremely
tough. And it also takes a while to learn it, because

00:27:38.690 --> 00:27:44.740
you have to defeat one nineball, then the
two nineballs before you even get a chance 

00:27:44.740 --> 00:27:55.260
to practice this fight. And those were hard enough so you don't get that many chances to practice.
But eventually I learned that Nineball Seraph

00:27:55.260 --> 00:28:00.159
always begins the fight by running at you
with his blade, which sends energy waves and

00:28:00.159 --> 00:28:06.110
requires a well timed boost-jump for you to
evade it. After that, he'll transform into this 

00:28:06.110 --> 00:28:11.250
little rocketship and zoom around the arena,
shooting missiles and trying to get behind

00:28:11.250 --> 00:28:17.360
you before transforming back into an attack phase
and just closing the distance with his blade and

00:28:17.360 --> 00:28:20.980
pulse rifle once again.
Over, and over, I tried to fight and defeat

00:28:20.980 --> 00:28:27.970
him. And over, and over, I lost.
But I had heard rumours of something. A controller

00:28:27.970 --> 00:28:33.960
technique, that might just be able to help
me. Something called the "Legendary armored

00:28:33.960 --> 00:28:40.840
core grip". Some said it was a meme. Others
said that by holding your controller backwards,

00:28:40.840 --> 00:28:46.840
you could get full access to every button
at once, which would allow for perfect control

00:28:46.840 --> 00:28:53.960
of your AC. So I gave it a try. 

00:29:14.720 --> 00:29:24.734
After countless attempts at phase one and about 16 victories in phase 2, I managed to overcome phase 3.

00:29:24.734 --> 00:29:28.369
With a little bit of skill, and a lot of luck.  

00:29:28.369 --> 00:29:33.078
And I was just joking about using the Armored Core grip. Or was I...?

00:29:33.078 --> 00:29:37.269
Anyway, nineball was dead. Thank god I don't

00:29:37.269 --> 00:29:44.150
have to fight that guy again.
Next up: Armored Core 2. I was excited for

00:29:44.150 --> 00:29:49.080
this, because this was a ps2 game, and I had
been eyeing that PS2 controller off to my

00:29:49.080 --> 00:29:55.370
side for a while now - knowing it had what
I wanted: dual analogue sticks. I couldn't

00:29:55.370 --> 00:30:02.179
wait to be able to aim properly..so I grabbed
the controller, moved the sticks and..they didn't work.

00:30:02.179 --> 00:30:09.409
What? For some unfathomable reason,
From Software had decided to stick with the

00:30:09.409 --> 00:30:15.960
back triggers for camera control in this game.
Which I just don't understand. The solution was

00:30:15.960 --> 00:30:21.280
right there! It's insane to me that this wasn't
a design priority for them. Maybe there's

00:30:21.280 --> 00:30:27.440
a good reason for this but I don't know.
Anyway - let's talk story: So Armored Core 2

00:30:27.440 --> 00:30:33.549
takes place 67 years after the events of Master
of Arena, when a cataclysmic event called

00:30:33.549 --> 00:30:40.140
the "Great Destruction" forced mankind underground.
Eventually however, mankind discovered the

00:30:40.140 --> 00:30:46.480
pre-war "Mars Immigration Project", and started
to set up colonies on the red planet - where

00:30:46.480 --> 00:30:52.460
wars over resources would inevitably break
out once more. It's a compelling story.

00:30:52.460 --> 00:30:59.460
But I wanted my story to begin in a unique
way. So this time, I didn't start with a single

00:30:59.460 --> 00:31:07.730
mission. Instead, I committed for some reason to 
playing exclusively in the Arena, until I had defeated

00:31:07.730 --> 00:31:12.549
every single opponent, and become the top
ranked raven.

00:31:12.549 --> 00:31:18.610
This ended up becoming an extremely memorable
experience for me. While the Arena doesn't

00:31:18.610 --> 00:31:24.039
incur repair and ammo costs, it doesn't exactly
give you many credits at the start either,

00:31:24.039 --> 00:31:30.139
so there was a limit to which parts I could
afford when attempting to outsmart my enemies.

00:31:30.139 --> 00:31:36.889
So for me, it was very important to read these
character descriptions before each fight..and

00:31:36.889 --> 00:31:42.890
I loved how much story and insight each one
revealed. Some characters were in the arena

00:31:42.890 --> 00:31:48.710
to pay off gambling debts...some were here
to show off their new parts for sale..and

00:31:48.710 --> 00:31:53.820
I distinctly remember #45 - who was a girl
piloting a mech in order to earn money to

00:31:53.820 --> 00:32:00.880
feed her starving family. Feels bad. But most importantly,
some descriptions revealed the enemy AI's

00:32:00.880 --> 00:32:07.679
preferred strategies and weaknesses. For example,
THIS guy was famously bad at energy management,

00:32:07.679 --> 00:32:11.850
and then there was another who would panic
if you pressured them. And

00:32:11.850 --> 00:32:17.139
some enemies were just pure comedy. At one
point, I met Bullet Dragon: a man who was

00:32:17.139 --> 00:32:21.840
in the arena because he loved the sound of
machine guns, and only fought so that he could

00:32:21.840 --> 00:32:28.380
afford more ammo. I couldn't outpace him with
my own machine gun, so I outlasted him and

00:32:28.380 --> 00:32:37.880
he flat out just decided to leave the arena
when he started to run out of machine gun ammo.

00:32:37.880 --> 00:32:42.870
And then there was Super Sonic,
who was a character who spent ALL his money

00:32:42.870 --> 00:32:49.370
on the fastest parts. Like..he was here to
build for speed, and nothing else. And then - poor guy -

00:32:49.370 --> 00:32:58.487
he went so fast that he accidentally flew
out of bounds in the arena, and I won. 10/10.

00:32:58.487 --> 00:33:03.441
It's in Armored Core 2's arena that I experimented
with basically every weapon type in the shop.

00:33:03.441 --> 00:33:09.770
there were so many ways to express
your creativity with the builds in this game, and constantly

00:33:09.770 --> 00:33:16.620
trying to exploit new enemy weaknesses taught
me so much about the pros and cons of various

00:33:16.620 --> 00:33:19.341
weaponry.
But the best lesson of all I learned from

00:33:19.341 --> 00:33:30.080
#19. AC name: mass. Pilot name: bulk. Guess what his build is? Yeah so his build was designed to just stay alive,

00:33:30.080 --> 00:33:37.390
while their opponents ran out of ammo. Which
I did. Many times. So in trying to overcome

00:33:37.390 --> 00:33:45.540
him, I found it: My new best friend - the
EWG-MGSAW: a machine gun with 1000 rounds

00:33:45.540 --> 00:33:53.370
of ammo, which allowed me to focus on one
strategy: unrelenting offense. With this gun,

00:33:53.370 --> 00:34:00.340
I flew towards top rank.
But on the way, I met Count One. Pilot name:

00:34:00.340 --> 00:34:07.370
No. 1111. Rank..11. "He's obsessed with the
number I and has put a great deal of effort

00:34:07.370 --> 00:34:12.860
into his current position. He'd rather be
ranked 11th than 1st, as it has twice as many

00:34:12.860 --> 00:34:21.300
1's. I realised even his weapon arms looked
like double 1's. You gotta respect that commitment.

00:34:21.300 --> 00:34:27.630
But in retrospect, his weapon arms would end
up being the key. Because my machine gun circling

00:34:27.630 --> 00:34:33.960
strategy ended up being useless against the
top ranked AC, Ares: who had the firepower

00:34:33.960 --> 00:34:40.419
and turning ability to completely counter
my strategy. So I built for power to overwhelm

00:34:40.419 --> 00:34:50.180
him instead. I took number 11's lead, and
used weapon arms, and absolutely destroyed

00:34:50.180 --> 00:34:55.780
Ares, to become #1. One one was enough for
me.

00:34:55.780 --> 00:34:59.980
Now I could actually start playing the game.
And when I finally started the missions, it

00:34:59.980 --> 00:35:05.840
didn't matter that my machine gun ammo was
extremely expensive. I was an arena MILLIONAIRE..

00:35:05.840 --> 00:35:10.099
And when I finally got to the last mission
and there was a sequence where I needed to

00:35:10.099 --> 00:35:14.650
fight enemies that couldn't be locked onto,
the arena had already taught me what to do.

00:35:14.650 --> 00:35:21.700
Instead of brute forcing the final fight with
sheer stubbornness this time, I actually used

00:35:21.700 --> 00:35:28.630
my shop knowledge to come up with a better
strategy. Equipped with rockets and grenade

00:35:28.630 --> 00:35:36.370
cannon arms that benefit from being manually
aimed, I made the final sequence WAY easier

00:35:36.370 --> 00:35:41.720
for myself.
The final boss's name is Leos Klein, and interestingly,

00:35:41.720 --> 00:35:48.950
it's implied that he was the player character
from Master of Arena. I love these little

00:35:48.950 --> 00:35:57.950
callbacks. There's a depth to the storytelling
here..though I'll admit: 99% of it is going

00:35:57.950 --> 00:36:03.849
over my head, as I'm so focused on the gameplay.
But even though I'm not paying much attention

00:36:03.849 --> 00:36:11.390
to the story - yet anyway..even I can tell
when it's missing. And nowhere did story feel

00:36:11.390 --> 00:36:15.820
more absent, than in Armored Core 2: Another
Age.

00:36:15.820 --> 00:36:22.130
The big selling point in this expansion seems to be the sheer amount of missions. I feel like

00:36:22.130 --> 00:36:28.430
most of these games had an average of between
20-30 missions before you hit an ending, but

00:36:28.430 --> 00:36:33.880
in Another Age, there are 100 missions in
the game. And whereas in some other games

00:36:33.880 --> 00:36:39.650
you can skip certain missions, or have them
go away if you fail - all 100 missions in

00:36:39.650 --> 00:36:46.450
Another Age are mandatory. Now, ima be honest:
every Armored Core game has its fair share

00:36:46.450 --> 00:36:52.339
of badly designed missions. Missions where
you get lost in endless corridors with unclear

00:36:52.339 --> 00:36:59.530
objectives. Missions where you accidentally
go out of bounds, or are forced to do platforming for some reason..

00:36:59.530 --> 00:37:05.770
And Another Age had tons of missions I hated.
But the worst in my opinion were missions

00:37:05.770 --> 00:37:11.560
that your AC could be fundamentally incompatible
with. For example, there was a mission here

00:37:11.560 --> 00:37:18.950
that required me to ascend a long corridor..and..I
just couldn't with so many of my builds. Don't

00:37:18.950 --> 00:37:26.020
get me wrong, I love reworking my AC to solve
combat puzzles, but having my build be fundamentally

00:37:26.020 --> 00:37:32.080
incompatible with some mandatory missions
was frustrating, to say the least. I guess

00:37:32.080 --> 00:37:36.890
this is why Armored Core 6 might be adding
jump pads, huh?

00:37:36.890 --> 00:37:44.680
But then there's a bright side to having mandatory
missions, in that it at least forces you to

00:37:44.680 --> 00:37:51.470
get good. For example, perhaps the most memorable
mission for me in Another Age was this one,

00:37:51.470 --> 00:38:01.770
where you have to fight not one, not two - but
THREE enemy ravens. All at the same time. And it's

00:38:01.770 --> 00:38:06.030
exactly as hard as it sounds.
One of the enemies is a heavy tank build that

00:38:06.030 --> 00:38:11.900
can harass you if you leave it alive. Another
is extremely mobile, and is equipped with

00:38:11.900 --> 00:38:18.440
powerful weaponry that can take you down VERY
fast. And the third AC luckily isn't much

00:38:18.440 --> 00:38:23.250
of a threat at all - though they still serve
as a distraction, and require you to spend

00:38:23.250 --> 00:38:28.750
precious ammo taking them down.
So once you have stock of your opponents,

00:38:28.750 --> 00:38:34.680
you have to come up with a strategy, and make
sure your AC design accommodates that strategy.

00:38:34.680 --> 00:38:39.630
And then you have to make sure you have the
skill to put strategy into practice.

00:38:39.630 --> 00:38:44.450
After a few attempts, I realised I had the
best success when I took out the tank AC first

00:38:44.450 --> 00:38:48.770
with machine gun fire, since they came at
me first, and aren't fast enough to dodge

00:38:48.770 --> 00:38:55.820
any of my projectiles. But fighting the other two was the
real challenge: because together they were

00:38:55.820 --> 00:39:02.310
difficult to split apart, and would grind me down
over time. So I needed a secondary weapon

00:39:02.310 --> 00:39:07.750
that could take one of them out quickly with
overwhelming power. I chose missiles as my

00:39:07.750 --> 00:39:14.710
secondary weapon, and I also equipped a "relation
missile" extension part. Once this extension

00:39:14.710 --> 00:39:24.531
was activated, it 
basically shot out more missiles as a bonus whenever I shot out my normal missiles, which essentially doubled my missile output for a

00:39:24.531 --> 00:39:31.010
short time, which was enough to deal severe
damage to one AC and finally even the odds.

00:39:31.010 --> 00:39:36.609
But then the final piece of the puzzle was
taking out the third AC. And since they were

00:39:36.609 --> 00:39:41.160
weak, this was simply an ammo issue. I had
a few attempts where I absolutely destroyed

00:39:41.160 --> 00:39:47.880
the first two, but simply did not have enough
ammo to finish the job. So frustrating. But

00:39:47.880 --> 00:39:52.550
eventually I made a build with two sets of
very ammo efficient missiles, and I grit my

00:39:52.550 --> 00:40:00.640
teeth as I took out the final AC on a sliver
of health, one missile at a time.

00:40:00.640 --> 00:40:06.660
I genuinely felt like I achieved something
here. It was very rewarding to have to discover

00:40:06.660 --> 00:40:10.800
the pieces of the puzzle on the battlefield,
solve that puzzle in the garage, and then

00:40:10.800 --> 00:40:16.210
put my solution to the test with my reflexes.
In my opinion, this is Armored Core at its

00:40:16.210 --> 00:40:23.010
best. And by the way, there's a part of Armored
Core 6's trailer that shows the player fighting

00:40:23.010 --> 00:40:30.000
three enemy ACs at once too. So..yeah. You
should prepare for that.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:34.660
But then it was back to the mission grind.
And credit where credit is due, the WAY you

00:40:34.660 --> 00:40:38.770
navigate all the missions in this game is
cool, because there are multiple missions

00:40:38.770 --> 00:40:43.981
for each region, and there's a good sense
of familiarity and progression in the world,

00:40:43.981 --> 00:40:47.836
as a result of that. And finally I made it to the end.

00:40:57.669 --> 00:40:59.670
 I'll be honest, I don't even know who that

00:40:59.670 --> 00:41:07.780
guy is. More than in any other Armored Core game, I actually
felt like an independent Raven in this one,

00:41:07.780 --> 00:41:13.910
just grinding away at mission after mission
on behalf of faceless corporations for credits

00:41:13.910 --> 00:41:22.280
alone.
So after spending so much time on this game,

00:41:22.280 --> 00:41:26.770
I was actually kind of appreciative at this
message Fromsoftware gives you at the end.

00:41:26.770 --> 00:41:32.340
"Thanks Raven. You've accomplished a great
feat. Your achievement is recognized by us.

00:41:32.340 --> 00:41:39.000
And then they give you a well-deserved reward.
MORE MISSIONS! Great...

00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:43.859
But to be fair, these missions were the best
of all. You face off against who I can only

00:41:43.859 --> 00:41:50.160
assume is Stinger from Project Phantasma,
with his being all glitchy and corrupted.

00:41:50.160 --> 00:41:55.890
I kind of like the flavour of these ancient ACs
just persisting throughout the ages, to eventually

00:41:55.890 --> 00:42:00.780
be fought as these corrupted remnants of another
age, later on.

00:42:00.780 --> 00:42:09.079
And of coruse, the real final fight is against
Nineball Seraph again. Because of course it

00:42:09.079 --> 00:42:17.320
is. And here, he fights the exact same way
as he does in Master of Arena..though the

00:42:17.320 --> 00:42:27.220
controls and visuals are a bit better here,
which is a relief. As then, he's extremely

00:42:27.220 --> 00:42:32.090
tough - but here, I discover a strategy that
will serve me well in future games. If you're

00:42:32.090 --> 00:42:37.119
ever facing an AC that tries to get behind
you - just back up against a wall. If fought

00:42:37.119 --> 00:42:46.260
in this manner, Nineball will chase you around
the edge of the arena, slashing at you with

00:42:46.260 --> 00:42:51.480
energy waves and shooting projectiles - which
you can dodge with another strategy I learned

00:42:51.480 --> 00:42:57.550
- which is to bunny hop boost tightly, left
and right. But even with all this, this fight

00:42:57.550 --> 00:43:04.390
was still hard as hell, and at this point
I'm amazed just how much From Software like

00:43:04.390 --> 00:43:12.160
toying with people's nineball PTSD by putting
him in so many games. But..I eventually won:

00:43:12.160 --> 00:43:15.400
and surely I won't have to fight that guy
again.

00:43:15.400 --> 00:43:19.780
And that's the second generation of Armored
Core games: complete. In retrospect, this

00:43:19.780 --> 00:43:26.599
might be the hardest generation of Armored
Core, if you take all of the missions together. Anyway, 

00:43:26.599 --> 00:43:32.530
next is Armored Core 3. A whole new generation!
Wouldn't it be great if they finally added

00:43:32.530 --> 00:43:43.440
dual analogue controls?
Yeah, nah..they still haven't made the right analogue stick useful which is..interesting - but at least there's a pretty good story

00:43:43.440 --> 00:43:48.940
this time. Armored Core 3 takes place during
the period of history where mankind was forced

00:43:48.940 --> 00:43:54.240
underground because of the Great Destruction.
Here, subterannean life is governed by "The

00:43:54.240 --> 00:43:58.300
Controller", an AI which..quite predictably
goes wild.

00:43:58.300 --> 00:44:03.770
The sequel, Silent Line, deals with the aftermath
of you destroying the controller. Mankind

00:44:03.770 --> 00:44:09.490
returns to the surface, and corporations begin
to fight over what lies beyond the "Silent

00:44:09.490 --> 00:44:17.810
Line". But mysterious technologies are already
up here on the surface, repelling their intrusions.

00:44:17.810 --> 00:44:25.050
These are compelling stories. This third generation
of games is very compelling in general actually.

00:44:25.050 --> 00:44:30.650
Controls and movement get tighter with every
gen, the way the shop and garage is integrated

00:44:30.650 --> 00:44:36.500
is really nice, menus are fast to navigate,
and I could be wrong: but I felt like weight

00:44:36.500 --> 00:44:42.770
limits were more generous in these games,
as I had less trouble fitting everything I

00:44:42.770 --> 00:44:48.800
wanted onto my AC.
That said, I'm not sure I have too much else

00:44:48.800 --> 00:44:55.230
to say about Armored Core 3 or its sequel,
Silent Line, that I haven't said already.

00:44:55.230 --> 00:44:59.559
As usual, there were good missions, and frustrating
missions - and the final fights were great

00:44:59.559 --> 00:45:06.030
fun - but I was never truly challenged by
anything in this game, which means I can't

00:45:06.030 --> 00:45:11.730
really point to any one thing that made these
games particularly memorable for me. Overall

00:45:11.730 --> 00:45:19.550
though, I think Armored Core 3 and Silent
Line are an excellent starting point for beginners,

00:45:19.550 --> 00:45:23.430
precisely because I don't think they're too
difficult, and because From Software had just

00:45:23.430 --> 00:45:27.339
added a ton of great QOL changes to the series
by this point.

00:45:27.339 --> 00:45:33.870
A cool new addition in this generation were
left arm parts that weren't just blades, or

00:45:33.870 --> 00:45:41.060
shields. So in this game you can equip your
left arm with bazookas, rifles..tons of really

00:45:41.060 --> 00:45:47.250
fun weaponry - these weapons fire with a delay, but
being able to fire two weapons at once can

00:45:47.250 --> 00:45:52.851
dramatically increase your damage input, and
it gives a really fun depth to builds. Only

00:45:52.851 --> 00:46:00.070
problem is..firing two weapons means you have
to mash two face buttons at once to fire them.

00:46:00.070 --> 00:46:04.963
And remember you're still boosting with the other face button so that's three face buttons you have to press at once. 

00:46:04.963 --> 00:46:09.731
And that's just a huge stress to your finger inputs on top of everything else, so because the series STILL

00:46:09.731 --> 00:46:17.619
refused to accommodate the right analogue
stick for aiming, dual firing was still uncomfortable

00:46:17.619 --> 00:46:24.550
with default controls, and I just opted for
a passive shield on my left arm instead.

00:46:24.550 --> 00:46:30.540
But FINALLY, the control scheme would be brought
into the modern ps2 era - with generation

00:46:30.540 --> 00:46:38.940
3.5 of Armored Core..which consisted of three
games: Nexus, Ninebreaker, and best of all:

00:46:38.940 --> 00:46:43.119
Last Raven.
With the ability to aim with the right stick

00:46:43.119 --> 00:46:49.280
added in Nexus, the L2 and R2 bumpers were
finally released from their torment as camera

00:46:49.280 --> 00:46:55.400
operators, and were able to be used for intuitive
things like boosting and switching weapons,

00:46:55.400 --> 00:47:01.300
instead. Which meant L1 and R1, instead of
strafing, could be used for firing left and

00:47:01.300 --> 00:47:08.460
right arm weaponry respectively. Which meant
dual wielding wasn't just viable, it was incredible.

00:47:08.460 --> 00:47:14.130
Dual firing added a new offensive depth to
the customisation that I was SO ready for.

00:47:14.130 --> 00:47:21.410
So I dived into the first mission equipped
with a new generator, and some fast boosters..and..immediately

00:47:21.410 --> 00:47:26.170
began to overheat.
Temperature management had always been a thing

00:47:26.170 --> 00:47:32.089
in the previous games. But back then, I only
really overheated when I was hit by too much

00:47:32.089 --> 00:47:38.290
enemy weaponry all at once, and it only did
a damage over time effect if I got too hot.

00:47:38.290 --> 00:47:44.069
In Armored Core Nexus: that all went out the
window. NOW I noticed my boosters contributing

00:47:44.069 --> 00:47:50.920
a LOT more to my heat meter, and if I overheated
in THIS game then my radiator would activate

00:47:50.920 --> 00:47:57.741
and suck up immense amounts of my own energy
in order to cool down my AC. And that is a

00:47:57.741 --> 00:48:03.829
LOT more impactful than just taking damage
over time. So all of a sudden, heat management

00:48:03.829 --> 00:48:11.110
had become a huge new consideration when considering
weaponry, generator, boosters and radiators.

00:48:11.110 --> 00:48:17.230
It's almost as if From Software realised their
game had just become easier with the new controls,

00:48:17.230 --> 00:48:24.099
and thought "we can't have that", and added
this insane overheat mechanic in revenge.

00:48:24.099 --> 00:48:29.530
Regardless: eventually I had my head around
this new heat system, so I started to experiment

00:48:29.530 --> 00:48:35.079
with new dual wielding setups. I tried double
chainguns..double sniper rifles.. handguns..

00:48:35.079 --> 00:48:41.119
laser rifles.. - but what I really fell in
love with was wielding two different weapons,

00:48:41.119 --> 00:48:47.069
rather than wielding two of the same. I ended
up settling on a bazooka and rifle combo.

00:48:47.069 --> 00:48:59.319
These two had similar range, but the rifle
was good for smaller, faster, or flying enemies,

00:48:59.319 --> 00:49:10.079
and the bazooka was great for the larger,
slower, or grounded enemies.

00:49:10.079 --> 00:49:16.680
That said, I ended Nexus with double bazookas..in
a fight against..a very familiar piece of

00:49:16.680 --> 00:49:23.560
ancient technology. But at this point, they'd
wheeled poor old nineball out so many times,

00:49:23.560 --> 00:49:27.829
that he's just not the same any more. Rest
in peace old friend.

00:49:27.829 --> 00:49:32.520
I guess it does make some sense that Nineball
would be here in this game though, as as far

00:49:32.520 --> 00:49:38.569
as I can tell, the story of Nexus is about
corporations fighting to control ancient technologies

00:49:38.569 --> 00:49:45.170
they don't understand. And it all backfires
at the end: Swarms of unmanned suicide drones

00:49:45.170 --> 00:49:51.640
spew out of a factory, and out onto the earth,
where they blanket the world in another sort

00:49:51.640 --> 00:49:57.720
of great destruction. It's awful, but it's
also one of the most memorable endings these

00:49:57.720 --> 00:50:04.319
games have ever had, just because of the phenomenal
way it's presented: You stand on the rooftop

00:50:04.319 --> 00:50:11.290
in the pouring rain, actually able to control
your character as this indomitable barrage

00:50:11.290 --> 00:50:18.920
of suicide drones ram into the earth's surface,
with you making your doomed last stand. There's

00:50:18.920 --> 00:50:25.210
no hope of you taking them all out, so you
simply fight on as the screen fades to black,

00:50:25.210 --> 00:50:32.390
and Nexus comes to an end.
So I enjoyed Nexus. But I..cannot say the

00:50:32.390 --> 00:50:37.450
same for the next expansion, which is Armored
Core: Ninebreaker.

00:50:37.450 --> 00:50:43.810
Get this: this game has no missions. It doesn't
even have a shop. At its core is is a training

00:50:43.810 --> 00:50:50.820
mode filled with 150 training exercises that
test you on things like movement, evasion,

00:50:50.820 --> 00:50:56.630
accuracy..stuff like that. Fun. That's..not really
Armored Core to me so I didn't really bother.

00:50:56.630 --> 00:51:02.770
So when I played, I figured the real point
of the game was the uniquely designed arena

00:51:02.770 --> 00:51:08.880
mode, which requires you to earn points and
climb that way, rather than just beating the

00:51:08.880 --> 00:51:13.530
next AC in your path.
But I made a terrible mistake with this game.

00:51:13.530 --> 00:51:23.109
I imported my save from nexus, which removed
almost all of the default parts from the garage,

00:51:23.109 --> 00:51:30.910
and replaced them with what I had in Nexus instead. And I didn't have many parts unlocked in Nexus, so I was

00:51:30.910 --> 00:51:38.530
essentially forced to do the entire arena..with
one build: A heavy bipedal with two bazookas.

00:51:38.530 --> 00:51:44.391
Luckily my build was strong enough to take
out 90% of my opponents, but there were some

00:51:44.391 --> 00:51:50.450
that were borderline impossible with my build.
To get to top rank by defeating the last guy,

00:51:50.450 --> 00:51:55.299
I had to wait until he challenged me on a
map where I could stand at the arenas edge

00:51:55.299 --> 00:52:00.896
so he couldn't fly behind me, and I JUST took
him out, after countless attempts.

00:52:00.896 --> 00:52:09.200
Maybe I'll do the training missions to prepare for Armored Core 6..and maybe they'd be great if you're a beginner to the series, but I wanted to move on because I 

00:52:09.200 --> 00:52:13.690
was SO excited for the next game. I had heard a lot about it. Because it

00:52:13.690 --> 00:52:19.360
was the last Armored Core game that had this
old-gen style, and I had heard it was perhaps

00:52:19.360 --> 00:52:28.740
the hardest game From Software had ever created.
Next was Armored Core: Last Raven..and it

00:52:28.740 --> 00:52:33.260
did not disappoint.
When we left off in Nexus, unmanned suicide

00:52:33.260 --> 00:52:38.920
weapons had ravaged the world. The corporations
lost everything. But instead of trying to

00:52:38.920 --> 00:52:44.580
rebuild individually, they joined forces to
form a new corporation named "alliance". But

00:52:44.580 --> 00:52:50.910
a rebel group known as "Vertex" vowed to overthrow
them. And war quickly escalated into chaos.

00:52:50.910 --> 00:52:57.150
Now Vertex has issued a declaration: their
final attack will launch in 24 hours. So it

00:52:57.150 --> 00:53:03.109
is that Last Raven starts with a clear story,
and a foreboding timer that counts down after

00:53:03.109 --> 00:53:08.520
every mission, building the tension.
Last Raven also perfects the mission structure,

00:53:08.520 --> 00:53:13.059
in my opinion, offering a set of missions
that, if done in the right order, push you

00:53:13.059 --> 00:53:18.990
towards different missions and endings. I
really liked being able to take a more active

00:53:18.990 --> 00:53:24.750
role in the story based on which path I chose.
And since so many of my best memories with

00:53:24.750 --> 00:53:31.770
Armored Core so far had been born from my
challenges, in this playthrough I opted to

00:53:31.770 --> 00:53:38.630
go down a story path that I had heard was
the hardest of them all. It's called the "Zinaida"

00:53:38.630 --> 00:53:43.849
path, named after your rival, who challenges
you in a ton of missions. So I knew it would

00:53:43.849 --> 00:53:50.760
GET hard. I was prepared for that. What I
wasn't prepared for, was for the first mission

00:53:50.760 --> 00:53:57.379
to literally be harder than anything I'd faced in the previous two games.

00:54:07.803 --> 00:54:13.059
Like..to put this into context..I had just
imported my build from Nine Breaker

00:54:13.059 --> 00:54:20.900
and this AC design with its dual bazookas had taken me through the
entirety of that game. It had taken me through

00:54:20.900 --> 00:54:27.680
the entirety of Nexus as well.
And yet I was struggling with the first

00:54:27.680 --> 00:54:35.630
mission with this build.
Eventually I took a step back, and realised

00:54:35.630 --> 00:54:41.099
I had to perfect my AC further if I was going
to take on these missions. So I took a few

00:54:41.099 --> 00:54:46.140
step backs actually, all the way back to the
first disc of Nexus, where I farmed credits

00:54:46.140 --> 00:54:51.440
for a few hours so that I could buy every
part in the shop. Then I went into the second

00:54:51.440 --> 00:54:58.450
disc of nexus, which had some optional parts
that I had missed..and in these missions I found an insanely powerful

00:54:58.450 --> 00:55:05.309
"Ananda" radiator to stop me from overheating,
and something called a "Linear Rifle", which

00:55:05.309 --> 00:55:11.210
was kind of like a halfway point between a
bazooka and a rifle. It had good stun capabilities,

00:55:11.210 --> 00:55:16.730
which helped my offhand weapon to hit more
reliably, and it would be at my side for the

00:55:16.730 --> 00:55:20.880
entirety of Last Raven.
I also discovered a type of missile called

00:55:20.880 --> 00:55:26.490
"Micro Missiles", which send a barrage of
smaller missiles out, making them great for

00:55:26.490 --> 00:55:33.420
overwhelming any enemies with anti-missile
systems built into their core, or extensions.

00:55:33.420 --> 00:55:40.559
With this setup, I was eventually able to
kite Bolt around the arena, staying just out

00:55:40.559 --> 00:55:45.829
of range of his cannons, while unloading everything
I had. Every circuit around the battlefield

00:55:45.829 --> 00:55:51.740
I would take time to hide and recover some energy behind
these buildings, and then I would start the circuit

00:55:51.740 --> 00:55:56.619
again. It was close, but I had completed the
first mission.

00:55:56.619 --> 00:56:05.260
Or at least, I thought I had. I didn't know what was going on.
I thought I had broken some crucial part of

00:56:05.260 --> 00:56:11.690
my AC here, and that the audio was glitching
out as a result. Because Last Raven DID

00:56:11.690 --> 00:56:18.840
add a new feature where you can break certain
body parts on your build during battle. So for a minute there, I thought

00:56:18.840 --> 00:56:24.292
this experience was VERY immersive. I was like - wow, my head part is broken and the audio is glitched, that's amazing.

00:56:25.882 --> 00:56:29.180
But my disk was actually scratched. That will 

00:56:29.180 --> 00:56:38.000
teach me to buy off of ebay. My mission flat
out refused to end, so I reset my PS2, managed

00:56:38.000 --> 00:56:41.331
to beat the mission again, and the same glitch
happened - but this time, at least, it let

00:56:41.331 --> 00:56:47.589
me finish the mission this time. Surely this won't be
an issue later, right?

00:56:47.589 --> 00:56:53.609
As was tradition whenever I knew I needed to get
stronger, I took on the arena, and to my surprise,

00:56:53.609 --> 00:56:58.740
I flew through almost all of it with no issue.
I even eventually added some heavy bipedal

00:56:58.740 --> 00:57:04.990
legs that I chose for their excellent boost
speed despite how heavy they were, which helped

00:57:04.990 --> 00:57:10.240
to offset the issues with my slow movement
- since I was just bunny hopping most of the

00:57:10.240 --> 00:57:16.530
time anyway. I would activate my EO core and
get these little floating turrets, then I'd

00:57:16.530 --> 00:57:22.579
unload all my micro missiles and my relation
missiles..then I'd purge all my missiles and drop them

00:57:22.579 --> 00:57:27.250
to lighten my load and increase
my speed a bit, before transitioning to linear

00:57:27.250 --> 00:57:34.640
rifle and bazooka and kiting my enemy around for
the win. Few stood a chance against this build, and

00:57:34.640 --> 00:57:39.720
so my enemies actually weren't too memorable as a result
- although - one of the funniest builds I

00:57:39.720 --> 00:57:48.520
faced in this arena was some guy with the hover extension part
who had built an infinite flight build. His

00:57:48.520 --> 00:57:57.280
build was pretty useless, and very easy to shoot at in the sky..but it was interesting. And 
I didn't know that was possible.

00:57:57.280 --> 00:58:03.770
But then everything became incredibly difficult, when I met
the guy at the top of the Arena:

00:58:03.770 --> 00:58:09.930
Daemon. And I think this guy might take the
title for being the best pilot I'd yet faced in

00:58:09.930 --> 00:58:16.930
these games. Just look at his technique! He's
a god.

00:58:16.930 --> 00:58:21.911
By combining a close range machine gun with
some sort of bazooka, Daemon is able to essentially

00:58:21.911 --> 00:58:28.210
stunlock you to death in no time at all. And
because he's so adept at flying at close range,

00:58:28.210 --> 00:58:33.270
it's an absolute nightmare trying to keep
him in your sights - even with the new analogue

00:58:33.270 --> 00:58:39.030
controls. I died to him over, and over, and
over - and felt true despair. Because this

00:58:39.030 --> 00:58:44.270
wasn't like previous games - I couldn't choose
the arena in Last Raven. I couldn't just put him in the

00:58:44.270 --> 00:58:51.050
garage and call it a day. No - this arena
was perfectly suited to his aerial strengths.

00:58:51.050 --> 00:58:56.000
I tried a few new things against Daemon. I
tried equipping an overboost core to help

00:58:56.000 --> 00:59:01.569
me get some distance, but my energy would
run out, and he would close in. I even tried

00:59:01.569 --> 00:59:08.240
a vertical FCS part for the first time, which gave me this tall lock box that was a bit better suited

00:59:08.240 --> 00:59:12.160
to Daemon's movement trajectory..but nope.
Not enough.

00:59:12.160 --> 00:59:17.030
I was about to give up and come back 
later...but then I had an idea. I thought

00:59:17.030 --> 00:59:21.650
back to that hover AC 
that I had laughed at a few hours ago..and

00:59:21.650 --> 00:59:25.609
wondered - what if he was the key? What if
I built something like that? If I could get

00:59:25.609 --> 00:59:31.980
up to the roof, and stay there - then I'd
be able to completely negate Daemon's aerial

00:59:31.980 --> 00:59:35.520
supremacy. I thought there was at least a
chance this would work, so I went into the

00:59:35.520 --> 00:59:41.210
garage with all my parts, and got to work.
Luckily, Last Raven includes the ability for

00:59:41.210 --> 00:59:47.079
you to tune most parts to be better in line
with your specific build requirements - but

00:59:47.079 --> 00:59:51.450
even so, this infinite flight build turned
out to be an extremely tricky one to make,

00:59:51.450 --> 00:59:56.760
but it taught me a lot. First, I needed the
build to be able to get me high into the air.

00:59:56.760 --> 01:00:02.010
So I chose reverse bipedal legs for their
superior jumping. Then I needed a generator

01:00:02.010 --> 01:00:07.721
with enough capacity, and boosters with enough
power to get me to the roof. Then I needed

01:00:07.721 --> 01:00:12.829
my build to be energy efficient enough to
keep my hover extension active, so I could

01:00:12.829 --> 01:00:19.099
STAY hovering. This meant I needed to choose
parts with low energy drain, as every part

01:00:19.099 --> 01:00:25.680
takes away from your generator's maximum output.
Eventually, I had a build that was so energy

01:00:25.680 --> 01:00:31.450
efficient it could float in the air AND recover
energy slowly as well. But THEN the final

01:00:31.450 --> 01:00:37.311
problem was to cram weaponry onto this thing..without
it weighing or draining me down. I needed

01:00:37.311 --> 01:00:42.840
weapons that were light, ammo efficient, and
with a lot of range. I eventually settled

01:00:42.840 --> 01:00:48.670
on micro missiles, and a sniper rifle. And
then, after so much trial and failure - I

01:00:48.670 --> 01:00:53.750
had it. I called this AC "Vulture", after
the way it sort of circles around its foes

01:00:53.750 --> 01:00:58.730
out of range..and it eventually took me to
victory against Daemon, with literally zero

01:00:58.730 --> 01:01:04.820
ammo to spare. I guess you could argue I cheesed
this fight, but it didn't feel like it.

01:01:04.820 --> 01:01:09.349
Armored Core's gameplay begins in the garage,
and I felt super proud of this win..because

01:01:09.349 --> 01:01:15.059
it felt like I had solved a really complex
puzzle. But for now, I put Vulture away..and

01:01:15.059 --> 01:01:20.319
pulled out my Blast Raven build instead..and
got back to the missions.

01:01:20.319 --> 01:01:24.900
By the way, that's another cool thing about
Last Raven: you're able to save and load different

01:01:24.900 --> 01:01:29.640
builds quickly. It's as if you have different
mechs just sitting in your hangar, just waiting

01:01:29.640 --> 01:01:34.089
to be taken out when it's their time to shine.
Being able to load specific builds

01:01:34.089 --> 01:01:38.440
is an amazing feature for these games to have,
as it stops you having to tinker with your

01:01:38.440 --> 01:01:43.970
build in the menus for ages. Instead, you
can build solutions. Name them. Get attached

01:01:43.970 --> 01:01:52.730
to them, and then be ready to switch up your
gameplay style whenever those builds are perfect for the situation at hand.

01:01:52.730 --> 01:01:57.450
Armored Core 6 had better have a system like
this, I swear. Even Elden Ring should

01:01:57.450 --> 01:02:00.890
have a system like this.
Another thing Armored Core 6 should take from

01:02:00.890 --> 01:02:06.950
Last Raven is its in-game lore repository,
which is just a great way for players to recap

01:02:06.950 --> 01:02:13.130
the story in-game. You know, this way they
don't have to rely on over-dramatic youtubers

01:02:13.130 --> 01:02:16.020
for all their lore. Wait maybe don't
put that in the ga-

01:02:16.020 --> 01:02:21.230
Last Raven's missions were a delight. Actually
the best I'd experienced so far. Almost every

01:02:21.230 --> 01:02:26.330
one was peppered with familiar enemy ACs that
would show up or betray you, and missions

01:02:26.330 --> 01:02:30.660
were just these great tests of your combat
efficiency, rather than having you run around

01:02:30.660 --> 01:02:35.770
the map for obscure objectives. That said,
there was one bad mission which plunged me

01:02:35.770 --> 01:02:42.359
into pure blackness after a maze of corridors,
making it borderline impossible to escape

01:02:42.359 --> 01:02:49.470
without a headpiece that had night vision.
Which I didn't have. So that was pretty lame.

01:02:49.470 --> 01:02:54.480
But throughout all of these missions, I had
been putting up with something very painful.

01:02:54.480 --> 01:02:58.330
Remember that glitch that showed up in my
first mission, where audio would bug out and

01:02:58.330 --> 01:03:06.160
the mission wouldn't end? Well that had been happening this whole time. And eventually, it became so bad

01:03:06.160 --> 01:03:10.890
that I found one mission that just wouldn't
end. Again, and again, and again, and

01:03:10.890 --> 01:03:15.950
again I completed the mission and it refused
to move on..until I gave up. I couldn't progress.

01:03:15.950 --> 01:03:23.130
I had to suck it up, put away the PS2..and
use an emulator to finish Last Raven, instead.

01:03:23.130 --> 01:03:27.961
Okay, so...you might be noticing a difference
in the quality of the visuals. Looking

01:03:27.961 --> 01:03:33.760
back, I kind of regret not trying emulators
sooner. This was amazing. With the right settings,

01:03:33.760 --> 01:03:38.619
Last Raven runs at a higher framerate, plays
at a sharper resolution, and I was even able

01:03:38.619 --> 01:03:43.950
to use save states to help me get back to
where I was in the missions faster. It hurt

01:03:43.950 --> 01:03:48.530
having to do ALL these missions again, but
I was enjoying the gameplay even more than

01:03:48.530 --> 01:03:52.501
before.
Listen. I love old hardware. And if

01:03:52.501 --> 01:03:57.839
I had just used emulators, then I might have
been tempted to use save states to overcome

01:03:57.839 --> 01:04:03.839
crucial moments of difficulty and tedium.
And If I had run these games at a silky smooth

01:04:03.839 --> 01:04:11.200
60fps and high resolutions, the games would
have been a lot easier and looked a lot better than they really are. I wouldn't

01:04:11.200 --> 01:04:15.910
have experienced them the same way everyone
else did all those years ago. But all that

01:04:15.910 --> 01:04:22.059
said, I'd still recommend emulating these
games over buying scratched, overpriced discs

01:04:22.059 --> 01:04:26.270
on Ebay. Ebay has atrocious buyer
support, and they won't let me get a refund

01:04:26.270 --> 01:04:31.950
for this scratched disc..so yeah, don't do that.
Anyway, eventually I made it to the final

01:04:31.950 --> 01:04:37.920
mission. A final gauntlet that first requires
you dodge through these long hallways filled

01:04:37.920 --> 01:04:48.539
with suicide drones. Then eventually, I actually got a checkpoint for the first time - so I didn't have to restart from the start anymore - then I had to take out some lasers

01:04:48.539 --> 01:04:53.880
on the walls. Then, it was the final fight with Zinaida.

01:04:53.880 --> 01:05:00.360
Here, she is widely regarded to be the most
difficult fight in the game. I'll let you

01:05:00.360 --> 01:05:02.634
watch, and figure out why:

01:05:14.295 --> 01:05:16.295
She fights a lot like Daemon - except I think

01:05:16.299 --> 01:05:23.980
she's even faster, fighting at more of a midrange
- with energy weapons that overheat you and

01:05:23.980 --> 01:05:30.500
somehow don't slow down her down at all.
Like all the best enemy ACs, I'm pretty sure she's flat out

01:05:30.500 --> 01:05:37.369
cheating, because there's no way you can build
an AC that could match her movement and firepower, right?

01:05:37.369 --> 01:05:41.249
Not to mention, you have to start this fight
at low health because of the suicide drones

01:05:41.249 --> 01:05:44.530
- unless you make it past the suicide drones
without taking damage.

01:05:44.530 --> 01:05:50.799
Which is what I did -  I spent ages learning the drone
patterns, and managed to somehow get to the

01:05:50.799 --> 01:05:55.299
checkpoint with full health.
THEN I just had to solve the Zinaida fight

01:05:55.299 --> 01:06:00.329
somehow. I knew I couldn't outrun her with
my heavy AC, so I came up with a new strategy:

01:06:00.329 --> 01:06:05.319
I realised that if I moved forwards and backwards
like this, then she would fly into my sights

01:06:05.319 --> 01:06:10.670
in a predictable manner..just long enough
for me to get off a salvo of missiles each.

01:06:10.670 --> 01:06:15.270
For the first time, I was actually dealing
consistent damage to her. But would it be

01:06:15.270 --> 01:06:18.750
enough? 
It required precise inputs, perfect aim, and

01:06:18.750 --> 01:06:25.670
by the end I was gripping my controller so
hard that my hands hurt. But then, at 500

01:06:25.670 --> 01:06:34.440
AP..I got it. The final shot.
The sun sets on you, as the Last Raven. Finally,

01:06:34.440 --> 01:06:41.359
I'd completed every game in the old generation
of games. Next was fourth gen, and it was

01:06:41.359 --> 01:06:47.299
was here, that the series would change forever.
For a new game director had entered the scene.

01:06:47.299 --> 01:06:51.190
That's right. Armored Core 4, and Armored

01:06:51.190 --> 01:06:56.309
Core For Answer are directed by none other
than Hidetaka Miyazaki himself.

01:06:56.309 --> 01:07:02.920
And in his new, VERY different fourth generation
of Armored Core, Raven pilots are no more.

01:07:02.920 --> 01:07:08.869
They've been superceded by LYNX, who pilot
NEXTS - new and improved Armored Cores that

01:07:08.869 --> 01:07:13.950
can move and fly and fight on a whole other
level. I mean..just look at how they fast

01:07:13.950 --> 01:07:17.299
they are!
The reason this combat is so much more intense

01:07:17.299 --> 01:07:23.160
is because a) it's now MUCH easier to attain
infinite flight and keep your boosters on

01:07:23.160 --> 01:07:29.819
all the time, and b) side boosts now have
a dedicated button, rather than being a niche

01:07:29.819 --> 01:07:35.119
extension part that only moved you in one
direction. What's more, cancelling your boosters

01:07:35.119 --> 01:07:41.300
and tapping side boost can allow you to perform
a quick-turn maneuver, giving skilled players

01:07:41.300 --> 01:07:47.420
the means to keep enemies in their sights
during frantic combat. Having these boosts

01:07:47.420 --> 01:07:52.359
built into the default controls was, in my
opinion, a huge step forward for the franchise

01:07:52.359 --> 01:07:58.311
- and of all the games, these are the ones
that I've replayed the most, having 6 full

01:07:58.311 --> 01:08:03.980
playthroughs under my belt, to date.
And while Armored Core 4 is great, and I really

01:08:03.980 --> 01:08:09.619
enjoyed putting together this energy weapon
build that dual wields pulse guns, only to

01:08:09.619 --> 01:08:14.539
throw them away when they're out of ammo to
reveal a backup set of two more pulse guns

01:08:14.539 --> 01:08:21.500
- most of my playthroughs are in actually
in 4's sequel, which is Armored Core: For

01:08:21.500 --> 01:08:26.529
Answer. Because I feel like it's here, in
this game, that the true potential of this

01:08:26.529 --> 01:08:33.569
generation was fully realised. Not just in
gameplay..but in storytelling, and tone as

01:08:33.569 --> 01:08:36.640
well.
So for the first time, I want to REALLY shout

01:08:36.640 --> 01:08:41.520
out the music in this game.
To be fair, the music in Armored Core has

01:08:41.520 --> 01:08:46.509
always been great. You've been hearing it
all throughout this video, after all. And

01:08:46.509 --> 01:08:52.500
a lot of credit for that seems to have to
go to a man named Kota Hoshino, who composed

01:08:52.500 --> 01:08:58.020
on almost all of these soundtracks. He even
runs a band called "FreQuency" whose music

01:08:58.020 --> 01:09:03.799
releases overlap almost entirely with the
Armored Core franchise and its distinct sound.

01:09:03.799 --> 01:09:10.710
And that's so cool. That Armored Core has
its own in-house band that plays for it! It

01:09:10.710 --> 01:09:17.609
also seems like Kota Hoshino has been working
with From Software ever since, so I hope his

01:09:17.609 --> 01:09:24.420
band creates some music for Armored Core 6.
Because the music was always good, but something

01:09:24.420 --> 01:09:30.819
specifically changed with Generation 4's music
- especially with For Answer, where many songs

01:09:30.819 --> 01:09:37.040
played that added this melancholy, nostalgic,
almost haunting sound to them.

01:09:37.040 --> 01:09:41.432
And it added such a good vibe to the franchise. I mean just listen to this!

01:09:41.432 --> 01:09:47.880
These sort of reflective tracks play in

01:09:47.880 --> 01:09:51.880
a lot of key missions. And I feel like they want you to ask the question: why? Why are we fighting? Are we

01:09:51.880 --> 01:09:56.580
really doomed to have all of this destruction
play out over and over again?

01:09:56.580 --> 01:10:05.190
And the answer is inevitably...yes. Despite
their promise, humanity is doomed. And in

01:10:05.190 --> 01:10:11.679
fact, Armored Core: For Answer has one of
the darkest, most disturbing endings that

01:10:11.679 --> 01:10:18.000
From Software has ever directed. And I want to show it to you.
Thing is, you can only get this unique ending

01:10:18.000 --> 01:10:24.469
if you complete the game THREE times. So that's
why I decided to replay For Answer so many

01:10:24.469 --> 01:10:30.550
times..and I'm so glad I did. Because these
NG+ missions are some of the best in the franchise,

01:10:30.550 --> 01:10:36.590
and replaying the game so many times helped
me to play closer to that insanely high skill

01:10:36.590 --> 01:10:42.530
ceiling that the fourth generation lays claim
to. S ranking certain missions rewarded me

01:10:42.530 --> 01:10:49.730
with new parts, and replaying so many times
also also gave me a TON of FRS points to invest,

01:10:49.730 --> 01:10:55.560
which are basically stat points that allow
you to level up and further tune the performance

01:10:55.560 --> 01:11:01.910
of your AC. By the end, I was overboosting,
side boosting and fighting with a speed and

01:11:01.910 --> 01:11:05.270
precision that I'm honestly quite proud of,
looking back.

01:11:05.270 --> 01:11:11.010
The reason my overboost looks so cool here
is because I'm using a core from the legendary

01:11:11.010 --> 01:11:16.929
White Glint, which gives it a unique animation.
White Glint is For Answer's cover character

01:11:16.929 --> 01:11:22.820
- a legendary character in this generation.
So, because I was taking parts from this mech,

01:11:22.820 --> 01:11:28.570
I jokingly called my NEXT "Black Flint"..and
actually ended up becoming pretty attached

01:11:28.570 --> 01:11:34.670
to this build, over the course of three playthroughs:
Black flint is equipped with dual machine

01:11:34.670 --> 01:11:40.320
gun arms, which fires both chambers with R1.
And despite these weapons being extremely

01:11:40.320 --> 01:11:45.170
powerful on their own, you're still allowed
to fire a back weapon at the same time - so

01:11:45.170 --> 01:11:50.989
I ended up equipping the "Yamaga" - a grenade
launcher that fires from the back here. And

01:11:50.989 --> 01:11:55.830
it's REALLY fun firing the machine guns constantly,
while always looking out for the perfect moment

01:11:55.830 --> 01:12:01.110
to fire a grenade. If my aim was good enough,
I could destroy enemies so quickly that their

01:12:01.110 --> 01:12:06.090
dialogue couldn't even finish in time.
Not to mention, the other parts I chose allowed

01:12:06.090 --> 01:12:11.330
for not only infinite flight, but basically
infinite side boosting as well, making me

01:12:11.330 --> 01:12:16.520
extremely difficult to hit. And then there's
my shoulder attachments, where I opted for

01:12:16.520 --> 01:12:20.840
the "Euphoria", which increased my Primal
Armor.

01:12:20.840 --> 01:12:26.980
But what is Primal Armor? Well, THIS is your
primal armour. It's a regenerating resource

01:12:26.980 --> 01:12:32.730
that primarily reduces the damage you take
- but it's also a resource you can dip into

01:12:32.730 --> 01:12:40.270
for overboosting, like this..or using assault
armor, like this. And It's important you understand

01:12:40.270 --> 01:12:45.750
this, because all signs indicate that Armored
Core 6 will have some successor to Primal

01:12:45.750 --> 01:12:52.590
Armor - since you can see it repelling attacks
here in the trailer..and you can see a similar

01:12:52.590 --> 01:12:58.180
assault armor explosion here, and here, as
well.

01:12:58.180 --> 01:13:03.380
Primal Armor is a technology based on "Kojima
Particles", which are extremely pollutive

01:13:03.380 --> 01:13:10.130
- to the point where primal armor is strictly
banned in inhabited areas, as the mere after-effect

01:13:10.130 --> 01:13:16.730
of a NEXT fighting in this space could contaminate
the area for generations to come.

01:13:16.730 --> 01:13:22.460
Now, a lot of our fights do take place near
high rise apartment buildings - but not to

01:13:22.460 --> 01:13:28.440
worry, they've almost always been long enveloped
by the ocean, or a desert wasteland. Sapped

01:13:28.440 --> 01:13:36.830
of colour, and life - after all, very little
of mankind now remains on the Earth's poisonous

01:13:36.830 --> 01:13:40.389
surface.
Instead, long ago, most of humankind fled

01:13:40.389 --> 01:13:46.679
to the cradles, which are these massive airship
colonies, each holding at least 20 million

01:13:46.679 --> 01:13:53.310
members of humankind, who escaped the pollution
below, and can live in the clean air above.

01:13:53.310 --> 01:14:00.070
But according to the revolutionary group called
ORCA, maintaining the Cradles is actually

01:14:00.070 --> 01:14:05.210
causing horrible pollution on the surface.
And it's ORCA's belief that that pollution

01:14:05.210 --> 01:14:10.650
will soon start bleeding into the atmosphere
itself, where the cradles fly.

01:14:10.650 --> 01:14:16.090
So ORCA seeks to commandeer the energy sources
powering the cradles, and use that energy

01:14:16.090 --> 01:14:21.821
for a different purpose. They wish to power
an array of anti-satellite canons, and turn

01:14:21.821 --> 01:14:27.909
them towards the sky, in an attempt to take
out an interstellar web of assault cells that

01:14:27.909 --> 01:14:37.230
are blocking mankind's escape to space. Because ORCA thinks this world is doomed.
In your first playthrough, you have no choice

01:14:37.230 --> 01:14:43.580
but to side with the League establishment,
who are attempting to prevent ORCA from taking away

01:14:43.580 --> 01:14:48.699
the Cradles energy source. The final fight
has you defend those energy sources here,

01:14:48.699 --> 01:14:52.719
in Arteria Cranium, where you fight against
two enemy NEXTS.

01:14:52.719 --> 01:14:54.634
So on your second playthrough, you'll find

01:14:54.634 --> 01:15:00.910
new missions to play, and these lead to the
ORCA ending, where you end up on the opposite

01:15:00.910 --> 01:15:07.300
side - attempting to wrest the power source
of the cradles from the establishment.

01:15:07.300 --> 01:15:12.360
In this ending, the cradles are forced to
land, subjecting mother and child to the pollution

01:15:12.360 --> 01:15:18.630
that they had escaped in the skies.
And even as humanity is decimated down here, they will

01:15:18.630 --> 01:15:21.760
discover a new frontier: outer space.

01:15:21.760 --> 01:15:27.429
But there's the third ending. One only open
to you if you take on ORCA members in their

01:15:27.429 --> 01:15:34.740
arena mode, until you meet and defeat an enemy
pilot known only as "Old King".

01:15:34.740 --> 01:15:41.580
He's a radical ORCA member who takes a...more
direct approach to things. And so, a new mission

01:15:41.580 --> 01:15:44.995
pops up on your board in this playthrough.

01:16:21.659 --> 01:16:29.190
This mission is easy. The only challenge to
you here is from your conscience, as Old King

01:16:29.190 --> 01:16:31.346
revels in the bloodshed.

01:17:00.203 --> 01:17:03.381
But then your reckoning arrives. In the last

01:17:03.381 --> 01:17:09.630
mission, you're led into an ambush against
a united force of foes disgusted with your

01:17:09.630 --> 01:17:15.670
actions. You and Old King have to face off
against FOUR enemy Nexts. And if you're doing

01:17:15.670 --> 01:17:21.440
this in Hard Mode, there's even a fifth. It's
an extremely challenging fight, even for my

01:17:21.440 --> 01:17:25.290
build, which I considered quite overpowered
until this point.

01:17:25.290 --> 01:17:31.150
But somehow I take out three enemy NEXTs..and
then the fourth gets stuck beyond the wall,

01:17:31.150 --> 01:17:38.961
separated from myself and Old King. Rather
than wait for my ally to get unstuck, I dive

01:17:38.961 --> 01:17:47.170
into combat on low health...and...just make
it out alive. It was nervewracking, but I

01:17:47.170 --> 01:17:51.130
felt like I came out of it a better player
- and I think one day I'd love to come back

01:17:51.130 --> 01:18:05.270
to attempt hard mode, as well. Things are harder in more interesting ways, like adding more mechs to the fights. So For 
Answer easily ranks within my top 3 AC games,

01:18:05.270 --> 01:18:10.670
alongside Last Raven and Master of Arena,
and I'd love to play it again.

01:18:10.670 --> 01:18:15.969
Now if it wasn't obvious already from the
footage - I emulated most of my For Answer

01:18:15.969 --> 01:18:23.230
playthroughs. That's because my disc wouldn't
play voice audio, for some reason. And I will

01:18:23.230 --> 01:18:28.960
say: being able to emulate this game without
any fps lag probably played a huge part in

01:18:28.960 --> 01:18:34.400
why I came to like this game so much, because
performance on the PS3 version is

01:18:34.400 --> 01:18:41.480
not on the same level. So many of these missions
are SO epic in scale - like taking out enormous

01:18:41.480 --> 01:18:49.010
arms forts, which should be incredible experiences
- but they put the old gen hardware to shame,

01:18:49.010 --> 01:18:52.489
unfortunately. Emulate these games, if you
can.

01:18:52.489 --> 01:18:57.710
That said, I've heard it's hard to emulate
the next couple of games on the list. And

01:18:57.710 --> 01:19:03.380
these are the last ones that I want to talk
about in depth here. Finally, we've arrived

01:19:03.380 --> 01:19:08.710
at the fifth, and for a long time - final
generation of Armored Core. It's time to talk

01:19:08.710 --> 01:19:13.100
about Armored Core 5, and its sequel, Verdict
Day.

01:19:13.100 --> 01:19:20.690
Where 4th gen's music was toned for a beautiful
melancholy, in 5th gen your garage sessions

01:19:20.690 --> 01:19:27.320
are instead accompanied by a thumping militaristic
beat that glorifies war - a theme that is

01:19:27.320 --> 01:19:31.420
absolutely reinforced by the gameplay and
story, as well.

01:19:31.420 --> 01:19:37.100
Because Armored Core 5 marks another huge
generational shift - except this time it's

01:19:37.100 --> 01:19:43.610
in the direction of making Armored Core more
of a mech war simulator, rather than the mechanised

01:19:43.610 --> 01:19:50.000
power fantasy that it was in the fourth generation.
...and while this new style would absolutely

01:19:50.000 --> 01:19:56.300
grow on me - especially in 5's sequel: Verdict
Day..I'm gonna to be honest and say I did

01:19:56.300 --> 01:20:01.520
not really enjoy my time with Armored Core
5. To be fair, this game was largely designed

01:20:01.520 --> 01:20:06.630
as a multiplayer experience - but there IS
still a singleplayer, and I don't think it's

01:20:06.630 --> 01:20:12.730
great. I found fps performance to be lacking,
I thought the visual clutter on the screen

01:20:12.730 --> 01:20:18.750
was atrocious, I didn't feel like I could
really outplay my enemies - and the missions

01:20:18.750 --> 01:20:24.880
were long and lackluster, with character dialogue
that kinda made me cringe.

01:20:24.880 --> 01:20:30.020
The most fun I had in this game was because
of my build: because for the first time really,

01:20:30.020 --> 01:20:36.320
I chose a really heavy tank build, loaded
with autocannons and heavy rifles. I figured

01:20:36.320 --> 01:20:40.920
if ever there was a time for a slow build,
it was in this generation, where I disliked

01:20:40.920 --> 01:20:48.110
the movement style. So doing less movement
suited me..and I will say: I loved the new

01:20:48.110 --> 01:20:54.230
feature that allowed me to charge into enemies
with my tank legs for massive damage. This

01:20:54.230 --> 01:21:00.020
is kind of like the move you can see the tank
build do in the Armored Core 6 trailer. So

01:21:00.020 --> 01:21:04.210
I had fun with my build - even if I didn't
like the game too much.

01:21:04.210 --> 01:21:09.070
But in the final mission of Armored Core V
- I saw a brief glimpse of how good the combat

01:21:09.070 --> 01:21:15.650
could be. In this fight, you have time to
pre-empt and dodge attacks, and learning the

01:21:15.650 --> 01:21:21.730
boss's moveset and attack patterns was quite
rewarding and..honestly something I hadn't

01:21:21.730 --> 01:21:25.960
really experienced much of before in Armored
Core.

01:21:25.960 --> 01:21:32.250
And luckily, 5's sequel: Verdict Day, clearly
learned a lot of lessons from 5. So instead

01:21:32.250 --> 01:21:38.321
of complaining about 5, I'd much rather talk
about what I enjoyed in Verdict Day, and properly

01:21:38.321 --> 01:21:43.530
explain the gameplay via my experiences with
that game, instead.

01:21:43.530 --> 01:21:49.230
Just like For Answer's soundtrack, Verdict
Day's songs are absolutely iconic. I mean..just

01:21:49.230 --> 01:21:56.840
listen to this beat
But it's not just the action tracks, the calmer

01:21:56.840 --> 01:22:03.151
garage and menu tracks are so memorable to
me now as well. So I'll let those play, while

01:22:03.151 --> 01:22:06.710
I take you through the garage of the fifth
generation.

01:22:06.710 --> 01:22:12.370
In fifth gen, you begin with basically one
part in every category, making it easy to

01:22:12.370 --> 01:22:18.170
try new weapons and equipment to try and figure
out what build you like. The catch? All of

01:22:18.170 --> 01:22:24.760
those parts are junk. Like, literally, they're
classified as "junk" parts, and are weaker

01:22:24.760 --> 01:22:29.960
than everything else that you can get in the
shop. But this is smart design, because it

01:22:29.960 --> 01:22:35.380
means that at the start of the game, you can't
really go wrong with anything you buy in the

01:22:35.380 --> 01:22:39.969
shop...because no matter what you do, it's
probably going to be an upgrade from what

01:22:39.969 --> 01:22:51.530
you have. It's a clever way to onboard new
players in my opinion, and it gives you a very clear sense of progression - especially at the start of the game, when that's very important, 

01:22:51.530 --> 01:22:57.179
and I wonder if they'll do something like this in Armored Core 6?
We do see a character scavenging parts from

01:22:57.179 --> 01:23:02.040
the battlefield at the start, after all.
The first mission to give me trouble in Verdict

01:23:02.040 --> 01:23:12.152
Day actually shaped my entire build for the rest of my playthrough - because I couldn't kill these guys 
guys, and the only solution that I could make work was sniping them. 

01:23:12.152 --> 01:23:14.610
And I was really happy to go with a sniper build because 

01:23:14.610 --> 01:23:20.770
sniping was another archetype I hadn't really
committed to properly, until now. I think

01:23:20.770 --> 01:23:26.179
it works well in this game because a) it's
very easy to get horizontal distance from

01:23:26.179 --> 01:23:33.090
your opponents...and b), the gameplay is just
generally more tactical in generation 5, with

01:23:33.090 --> 01:23:43.540
great rewards for careful positioning, which only got better given I could snipe enemies from really far away. So
let me explain the combat.

01:23:43.540 --> 01:23:48.760
In this generation, your boosters can be toggled
on or off, allowing you to either glide or

01:23:48.760 --> 01:23:53.280
walk. And if you're gliding on the ground,
then you're able to activate Glide Boost,

01:23:53.280 --> 01:23:59.100
which is essentially an overboost that moves
you around at an extreme speed, at the expense

01:23:59.100 --> 01:24:04.540
of more energy. Then finally there's quick
boost, which is kind of like the side boost

01:24:04.540 --> 01:24:10.800
from Armored Core 4..but..worse.
And then I guess you could also include jumping

01:24:10.800 --> 01:24:17.650
in this discussion..because jumping is ALSO
extremely important in this generation, as

01:24:17.650 --> 01:24:28.159
jumping up buildings is your primary means
of gaining height over your opponents, and

01:24:28.159 --> 01:24:35.720
jumping perpendicular from them is one of
the fastest ways to move horizontally.

01:24:35.720 --> 01:24:41.710
Looking back at it, honestly it's absurd that
there are four separate buttons just for moving

01:24:41.710 --> 01:24:46.030
around the map in this game. Armored Core
has always been a series that stresses the

01:24:46.030 --> 01:24:52.110
amount of controller inputs, but the fifth
generation really takes it too far, in my

01:24:52.110 --> 01:24:54.900
opinion.
Not to mention, another essential part of

01:24:54.900 --> 01:25:01.090
combat is "Scan mode", which you can toggle
on and off with R3. In this mode, you can't

01:25:01.090 --> 01:25:07.820
fire your weapons - but your energy output
skyrockets, allowing builds to recover energy

01:25:07.820 --> 01:25:13.360
quickly in scan mode, and to be way more evasive
for a long period of time. I would use this

01:25:13.360 --> 01:25:18.020
whenever I needed to get distance from my
enemies, then I'd go back into combat mode

01:25:18.020 --> 01:25:21.910
for sniping.
Of course, the other use for scan mode is..you

01:25:21.910 --> 01:25:27.780
know: scanning, which is also very important.
That's because there are three damage types

01:25:27.780 --> 01:25:33.330
in the fifth generation: Kinetic, Thermal,
and Chemical. And in retrospect, it's essential

01:25:33.330 --> 01:25:38.409
that you scan enemies: especially bosses,
and it's essential you choose weaponry that

01:25:38.409 --> 01:25:44.590
can overwhelm their unique defenses. Otherwise
you'll see the words "innefective" whenever

01:25:44.590 --> 01:25:51.260
you hit them, and your weapons will just tickle
them instead. Now I was lazy and rarely felt

01:25:51.260 --> 01:26:00.230
like scanning, so there were a lot of enemies
I just tickled to death instead: I'll admit..I don't love the scan system. Because while 

01:26:00.230 --> 01:26:06.619
I LOVE when Armored Core forces you to reconsider
your strategy and experiment with things,

01:26:06.619 --> 01:26:13.489
but there's not much creativity involved when
you're forced to just choose a part enemies

01:26:13.489 --> 01:26:21.719
are weak to. So I didn't enjoy this damage
system much.

01:26:21.719 --> 01:26:27.420
What I DID like in this game was the addition
of UNACS, which are these AI companions you

01:26:27.420 --> 01:26:33.250
can choose to bring with you to every mission.
There's some fun strategy involved in choosing

01:26:33.250 --> 01:26:39.270
a complementary ally - for example I often
chose a UNAC that would tank and take aggro

01:26:39.270 --> 01:26:45.730
from me while I sniped in the distance - and
it made this game WAY more enjoyable. In the

01:26:45.730 --> 01:26:55.434
previous game I just felt like a bullet sponge
that couldn't evade properly, but here the UNACS actually enabled a lot of strategy, which is great. 

01:26:55.434 --> 01:27:01.647
But even UNACS can only do so much against the final boss, 

01:27:01.647 --> 01:27:11.659
Who was perhaps unsurprisingly the most memorable part of Verdict Day for me. Because I spent a LOT of time

01:27:11.659 --> 01:27:16.260
trying to figure this guy out.
He's essentially piloting a NEXT from Armored

01:27:16.260 --> 01:27:22.030
Core 4 - and it's terrifying to go against.
It makes you realise just how powerful those

01:27:22.030 --> 01:27:29.090
things were back then. Its strength is its
primal armor, which is almost impossible to

01:27:29.090 --> 01:27:35.560
break through - until it goes in for assault
armor, that leaves it vulnerable for a short

01:27:35.560 --> 01:27:40.230
moment. I eventually figured out that if I
dashed in and took a bit of damage here, I

01:27:40.230 --> 01:27:49.160
could end phase one in an instant. Because
I was using dual pile drivers - a short melee

01:27:49.160 --> 01:27:55.070
weapon that essentially rams a warhead deep
into the body of your opponent for insane

01:27:55.070 --> 01:28:01.500
damage. It's very similar to what you see
here in the AC6 trailer, actually.

01:28:01.500 --> 01:28:08.840
Then phase 2 begins, and you have to play
out of your mind to succeed - activate scan

01:28:08.840 --> 01:28:17.989
mode overboost to evade those missiles. Deal
damage whenever he stands still at the correct

01:28:17.989 --> 01:28:24.290
range. Try to keep the sun behind you because
for the love of god that sun is obnoxious.

01:28:24.290 --> 01:28:29.889
This fight is LONG, and you'll take a lot
of unavoidable damage, so you'd better play

01:28:30.000 --> 01:28:35.201
perfectly in order to deal enough damage in
time..and take him out.

01:28:35.201 --> 01:28:38.040
Overall, I enjoyed Verdict day just as much

01:28:38.040 --> 01:28:42.880
as any other Armored Core game. With it, they
really perfected a lot of design decisions,

01:28:42.880 --> 01:28:51.040
and it goes to show how the sequels are sometimes
the best of each generation, rather than just

01:28:51.040 --> 01:28:59.080
being more of the same.
But I'm not done yet. Because last frontier

01:28:59.080 --> 01:29:05.640
was this. Formula Front: Extreme Battle, on
the PSP. I never had a PSP when I was younger,

01:29:05.640 --> 01:29:10.250
so I wasn't going to pass up the opportunity
to buy one.

01:29:10.250 --> 01:29:16.330
There are four Armored Core games on the PSP,
but three of these - AC 3, Silent Line, and

01:29:16.330 --> 01:29:21.280
Last Raven - these are remakes.
The unique PSP title is this one: Formula

01:29:21.280 --> 01:29:26.270
Front, Extreme Battle - where you're given
every single part from the get-go, and are

01:29:26.270 --> 01:29:32.940
simply tasked with climbing the ranks in the
arena. The catch here, is that you can technically

01:29:32.940 --> 01:29:39.720
beat this entire game without actually playing
- as there's a whole system of AI coding where

01:29:39.720 --> 01:29:47.040
you calibrate your AC to fight for you. It
sounds good in theory, but these AI menus

01:29:47.040 --> 01:29:52.431
take forever to navigate, and often your AC
won't listen to your preset commands, and

01:29:52.431 --> 01:30:01.030
will just happily shoot missiles into a wall
instead. So I ended up playing manually, and

01:30:01.030 --> 01:30:06.820
I will say: the enemy ACs in this arena are
designed extremely well. Their builds are

01:30:06.820 --> 01:30:12.320
so unique that I ended up being forced to
make four drastically different builds in

01:30:12.320 --> 01:30:15.360
order to progress, and counter them.

01:30:15.360 --> 01:30:16.760
 It was fun.

01:30:16.760 --> 01:30:21.489
One funny thing is the whole aesthetic of
this game. While every other game is this

01:30:21.489 --> 01:30:27.800
dystopian commentary on the terror of war
and the dispassion of business, the music

01:30:27.800 --> 01:30:34.690
and intros in Formula Front make you feel
like you've entered a race in gran turismo,

01:30:34.690 --> 01:30:40.429
with an audience of adoring fans watching
in anticipation as the announcer shouts "GO"

01:30:40.429 --> 01:30:47.980
with glee. It's..weird. If this is what peacetime
looks like in the Armored Core universe, then

01:30:47.980 --> 01:30:51.770
I'll take the dystopian corporate nightmare,
please.

01:30:51.770 --> 01:30:57.580
So in conclusion..I like Armored Core. And
some of these games..I even loved - again

01:30:57.580 --> 01:31:02.739
my top 3, in the end, were Master of Arena,
Last Raven, and For Answer.

01:31:02.739 --> 01:31:08.210
And I'm very thankful that I have you all,
and this channel, as it motivated me to push

01:31:08.210 --> 01:31:13.780
through and understand this franchise. Because
of that, I discovered a genre of game that

01:31:13.780 --> 01:31:19.320
I otherwise might have never discovered. And
I think it's a shame that so many others haven't

01:31:19.320 --> 01:31:24.100
tried these games. So I hope this video might
help to convince a few people to give them

01:31:24.100 --> 01:31:30.679
a try, and to push through any hardships they'll
face. So that you can get some mechanized

01:31:30.679 --> 01:31:34.970
memories of your own, so to speak.
So again: if you wanted to play anything from

01:31:34.970 --> 01:31:39.881
this series..either see if you can get a fair
price for them second hand, or emulate. And

01:31:39.881 --> 01:31:46.659
if you do emulate: be prepared for some technical
fiddling with settings, but it should all

01:31:46.659 --> 01:31:53.820
be pretty possible with guides online. 

01:31:53.820 --> 01:32:01.400
And if you are interested, well, then which

01:32:01.400 --> 01:32:06.630
games should you play? Well..that's actually
not an easy question to answer.

01:32:06.630 --> 01:32:11.380
If you only have time to play one Armored
Core game, then I'd recommend starting in

01:32:11.380 --> 01:32:17.390
the fourth generation, with Armored Core:
For Answer. It's probably the easiest single

01:32:17.390 --> 01:32:25.159
game to get to grips with, and it has fantastic
depth if you decide to do multiple playthroughs.

01:32:25.159 --> 01:32:29.480
If you have time to play a couple of games,
then I'd recommend you play Armored Core 3,

01:32:29.480 --> 01:32:35.350
and Silent Line. These felt quite accessible
to me. But if you REALLY want dual analogue

01:32:35.350 --> 01:32:42.150
controls and a challenge, then I'd recommend
both discs of Armored Core Nexus, and then

01:32:42.150 --> 01:32:47.190
Last Raven instead.
But of course, this is all just my opinion.

01:32:47.190 --> 01:32:53.300
Play any from this video that sound interesting
to you. You can't go too wrong if you're dedicated

01:32:53.300 --> 01:32:59.150
enough. Okay, maybe don't play Ninebreaker.
But that's about it.

01:32:59.150 --> 01:33:07.400
But thank you for listening to this extremely
long video..I hope it gets you as excited

01:33:07.400 --> 01:33:12.770
for AC6 as I am..and I'll see you next time.

